          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Lech Wałęsa</strong><br>\nLech Wałęsa was born on September 29, 1943, in Popowo, Poland. Growing up in a war-torn country, his early life was marked by the hardships of World War II. Despite the challenges, he completed vocational training and became an electrician. His work at the Gdańsk Shipyard would later become a significant part of Polish history.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Gdańsk Shipyard Strike</strong><br>\nIn 1980, Wałęsa led a strike at the Gdańsk Shipyard to demand better working conditions and pay. The strike grew into a nationwide movement against the communist regime. Wałęsa's leadership in this movement helped to establish the first independent trade union in the Soviet bloc, known as Solidarity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Solidarity Movement</strong><br>\nThe Solidarity movement, under Wałęsa's guidance, became a symbol of resistance against communist rule. It gained immense support from workers and intellectuals. The government tried to suppress the movement by imposing martial law in 1981, but Solidarity remained an important force for change.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
