          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Nobel Peace Prize</strong><br>\nIn recognition of his leadership and peaceful resistance efforts, Wałęsa was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 1983. He was honored for his commitment to worker's rights and his struggle for freedom through non-violent means. Wałęsa's wife, Danuta, accepted the prize on his behalf since he feared he would not be allowed to return to Poland if he left the country.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Round Table Agreements</strong><br>\nIn 1989, Wałęsa participated in the Round Table Talks with the communist government. These discussions led to significant changes, including legal recognition of Solidarity, the introduction of democratic elections, and eventually the fall of communism in Poland.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Presidency</strong><br>\nLech Wałęsa served as the President of Poland from 1990 to 1995. His presidency was pivotal in transitioning Poland from a communist to a democratic state. He implemented reforms that shifted Poland towards a market economy and sought to strengthen ties with the West.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
